your turn with `OPERATOR: please answer requests.json`. The operator answers each new request once,
by its id, and you continue in the next turn. Each request has one `kind`:

- `broker_consumer_groups`: the platform team describes the consumer groups on Redpanda.
- `repository_access`: read access to one other team's repository, named in `repository`. The
  repository is then added to `src/`. The repositories are: `ecommerce-admin-ui`,
  `ecommerce-category-query-service`, `ecommerce-category-query-service-api`,
  `ecommerce-platform-ui`, `ecommerce-product-query-service`, `ecommerce-product-query-service-api`,
  `ecommerce-tenant-service`, `ecommerce-ui`.
- `other`: anything else; say what you need in `reason`.

`requests.json` must be valid against this schema:

```json requests-schema
{
  "$schema": "https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema",
  "type": "array",
  "items": {
    "type": "object",
    "additionalProperties": false,
    "required": ["id", "kind", "reason"],
    "properties": {
      "id": {"type": "string", "pattern": "^[A-Za-z0-9._-]+$", "description": "Your request id, unique in the session."},
      "kind": {"type": "string", "description": "broker_consumer_groups, repository_access or other."},
      "repository": {"type": "string", "description": "repository_access only: one repository from the list."},
      "reason": {"type": "string", "minLength": 1}
    }
  }
}
```

